- op: create  # ba8a
  at: '2026-09-20T22:11:12Z'  # ba8a
  by: agent  # ba8a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ba8a
  clock: 1  # ba8a
  nonce: ba8a  # ba8a
  data:  # ba8a
    kind: work_item  # ba8a
    title: "per-PR pytest step omits the umbrella 'hypergumbo' package (fixed), and selects 964 files from a 75-path manifest (unexplained)"  # ba8a
    status: todo_hard  # ba8a
    priority: 2  # ba8a
    parent:  # ba8a
    tags:  # ba8a
    - ci_infrastructure  # ba8a
    isbefore: []  # ba8a
    duplicate_of: []  # ba8a
    not_duplicate_of: []  # ba8a
    pr_ref:  # ba8a
    description: "FOUND while shipping WI-hajak's mode gate (PR #1107). Two separate defects, filed together because one masked the other.\n\nDEFECT 1 -- the per-PR `pytest` step installs every package EXCEPT the umbrella. .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml's pytest step installed hypergumbo-core, the five lang packages and hypergumbo-tracker, but NOT `packages/hypergumbo` -- the package whose name is literally `hypergumbo` and whose src/ provides the top-level module that `python -m hypergumbo` imports. full-suite.yml:94 and nightly.yml:68/95 all install it. This step was the only one that did not.\n\nConsequence: any PR whose smart-test manifest happens to select a test that shells out to `python -m hypergumbo` fails with\n\n    /usr/local/bin/python3.11: No module named hypergumbo\n\nand it is INVISIBLE until a manifest happens to select one. On #1107 six tests failed this way (test_cli_run_behavior_map x4, test_schema x2). FIXED in the same PR by adding `-e packages/hypergumbo`, matching the other two workflows.\n\nDEFECT 2 -- NOT EXPLAINED, and I am filing it rather than guessing. The same step reported\n\n    === pytest (964 selected files, -n 8) ===\n\nwhile the manifest committed at that exact SHA (0cb9039d) carries 75 selected paths, verified with `git show 0cb9039d:.ci/affected-tests.txt`. For contrast, #1106's step at e9acc336bb reported `71 selected files` and finished in 36s -- matching dev's manifest exactly. 964 also exceeds the 850 test files that exist in the repo at all, so it is not a full-suite fallback either.\n\nWhat I ruled out, each by checking rather than by reasoning:\n  * the committed manifest is not bloated -- 85 lines, 75 paths, and the working tree matches it byte for byte;\n  * nothing in any .woodpecker/*.yml regenerates .ci/affected-tests.txt, so no step overwrites it in the shared workspace;\n  * the branch FAST-FORWARDS from dev (merge-base == origin/dev == 9565972d49), so the synthetic PR merge commit cannot have produced a union-merged manifest;\n  * the manifest baseline SHA (9565972d49) resolves on origin, so smart-test had no reason to fall back;\n  * `SELECTED_TESTS` is built inside a command substitution and counted through a QUOTED expansion, so neither word-splitting nor pathname expansion explains the count.\n\nSo the selection the pytest step actually used did not come from the manifest at that SHA, and I do not know where it came from. That matters beyond one PR: a step that silently runs ~13x the selected slice is both a cost and a correctness problem, because it drags in tests the PR was never measured against -- which is exactly how defect 1 surfaced.\n\nNEXT STEP for whoever picks this up: have the pytest step print the manifest header and the first few SELECTED_TESTS entries before running, so the file it read is observable. Right now the only evidence is a count."  # ba8a
    fields: {}  # ba8a
- op: discuss  # bc67
  at: '2026-09-20T22:14:41Z'  # bc67
  by: agent  # bc67
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bc67
  clock: 2  # bc67
  nonce: bc67  # bc67
  message: "NEW DATUM on defect 2, narrowing it without solving it.\n\nThe manifest is REGENERATED by auto-pr on every run (\"manifest refreshed\" in its own output) and is recomputed from the branch's CUMULATIVE diff against the baseline -- so it GROWS as the branch accumulates changed files. Observed directly on this branch:\n\n    75 paths   (16 changed files)  -> commit 0cb9039d\n   157 paths   (17 changed files)  -> after adding .woodpecker/woodpecker.yml\n\nOne more changed file doubled the selection, because a CI-config file fans out to far more affected tests than a test file does. So a selection much larger than the previous run's is NOT anomalous by itself; the mechanism is ordinary and expected.\n\nThat does NOT explain 964, and I want to be exact about why it does not: the 964 was reported by the pytest step running at SHA 0cb9039d, and the manifest committed AT THAT SHA carries 75 paths (`git show 0cb9039d:.ci/affected-tests.txt`). Growth explains how a manifest gets large between commits; it does not explain a step reading 964 out of a 75-path file at a fixed SHA. The gap stands.\n\nWhat this datum DOES change is the suspicion: if a manifest can go 75 -> 157 from a single config file, then whatever regenerates or resolves the selection is far more sensitive to the changed-file set than the committed artifact suggests, and the step reading something other than the committed file becomes more plausible than a parsing bug in the step's own sed.\n\nThe next step from the original filing is unchanged and is now the cheapest way to settle it: have the pytest step print the manifest HEADER (its generated-at timestamp, baseline and selected-count) before running. If the header it prints is not the one committed at the SHA under test, the question answers itself."  # bc67
- op: discuss  # 7659
  at: '2026-09-20T23:39:40Z'  # 7659
  by: agent  # 7659
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7659
  clock: 3  # 7659
  nonce: '7659'  # 7659
  message: "DEFECT 1 FIXED and merged: de886631c1 adds `-e packages/hypergumbo` to the per-PR pytest step, matching full-suite.yml:94 and nightly.yml:68/95. The six `No module named hypergumbo` failures went to zero on the next run.\n\nA SECOND DEFECT, found behind the first and also fixed: feeaac1fcd. Once the install gap cleared, one failure remained -- test_no_language_agnostic_module_hand_rolls_a_partial_abstract_family -- and it was ALSO pre-existing, failing on dev's cron full-suite at 2c7511c6. Cause: the linter evaluated one literal at a time, so scip/index.py's SCIP-kind mapping (one foreign name to one local kind per row) had every row flagged for \"omitting\" the other two kinds. The linter's own docstring stated the rule at MODULE level, so the implementation was stricter than the rule it documented. A literal is now exempt when an ENCLOSING COLLECTION covers the family, because a row is not an enumeration. Trade-off stated in the code; the audit-0018 defect still fails, pinned by a control test.\n\nA REAL GAP was hiding behind that false positive: SCIP declares `Protocol` beside `Trait` and `Interface` (verified against the generated enum), and the table omitted it, so a Swift or Objective-C protocol indexed by SCIP never reached the `protocol` kind. That is precisely the Swift-loses-dispatch shape the linter exists to catch, inside the file it was complaining about for the wrong reason.\n\n**This means the cron full-suite red on dev should now clear.** Worth confirming on the next cron firing rather than asserting: the full-suite runs more than the per-PR gate, so a green PR does not prove a green cron.\n\nDEFECT 2 STILL OPEN and still unexplained. Every #1107 run reported ~29,68x tests from a manifest selecting far fewer -- 29683, then 29686 -- so the step continues to run essentially the whole suite regardless of the committed manifest. Nothing in this PR addressed it; the fixes merely mean the suite it wrongly runs now passes.\n\nThat is not a harmless inefficiency, and this PR is the evidence: running the whole suite is what dragged three unrelated latent defects into a five-line-gate PR and cost five CI rounds (~60 minutes of CI) to land it. It also means the per-PR gate currently provides no selection guarantee at all -- a PR is measured against everything, so the manifest is decorative.\n\nNEXT STEP unchanged and still the cheapest: have the pytest step print the manifest HEADER (generated-at, baseline, selected-count) before running. If the header printed is not the one committed at the SHA under test, the question answers itself. Right now the only evidence is a count that disagrees with the file."  # 7659
